  "co2WhatTitle": "What does this number mean?",
  "co2WhatBody": "When your factory uses electricity, gas or diesel, carbon dioxide (CO₂) gas goes into the air. This gas makes the earth hotter. The number shows how much your factory caused.\n\n• t = tonne. 1 tonne = 1,000 kg.\n• CO₂e means \"CO₂ equivalent\": other warming gases are counted as the same amount of CO₂.\n• Scope 2 = from the grid electricity you buy. Power plants burn gas and oil to make it.\n• Scope 1 = from fuel your factory burns itself: diesel generator, gas boiler.\n\nHow it is calculated: units on the bill × emission factor. Example: 1,000 kWh × 0.62 kg per kWh = 620 kg = 0.62 t CO₂e.\n\nForeign buyers ask for this number. Lower is better: saving electricity or using solar brings it down.",
  "co2Explain": "What does this mean?",
